University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Sex-dimorphic neuroprotective effect of CD163 in an α-synuclein mouse model of Parkinson's disease","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-099.09-716129","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"The association between assisted reproductive technologies : (ARTs) and the body mass index (BMI) of children remains controversial. Confounding by morbidity and other factors associated with parental infertility may have biased studies comparing children born after ART with children born after no treatment. We investigated the associations between different fertility treatments and BMI in children at age 5 to 8 years, adjusting for and stratifying by causes of parental infertility. Methods and findings This Danish cohort study included 327,301 children born between 2007 and 2012 (51% males, median age at follow-up 7 years). Of these, 13,675 were born after ART, 7,728 were born after ovulation induction with or without intrauterine insemination [OI/IUI], and 305,898 were born after no fertility treatments. Using the International Obesity Task Force (IOTF) standards, we defined overweight (BMI ≥ IOTF-25) and obesity (BMI ≥ IOTF-30). We compared children born after ART versusAU OI/IUI; intracytoplasmic sperm injection ; }vs:}shouldbespelledo (ICSI) versus conventional in vitro fertilization (IVF); and frozen-thawed versus fresh embryo transfer and estimated crude and adjusted prevalences of children with overweight or obesity at age 5 to 8 years, prevalence odds ratios (PORs), and differences in mean BMI z-scores. Adjustment was performed using stabilized inverse probability of treatment weights, including parity, year of conception, parental causes of infertility, age, educational level, comorbidities, maternal country of origin, BMI, and smoking as covariates. The crude prevalence of obesity was 1.9% in children born after ART, 2.0% in those born after OI/IUI, and 2.7% in those born after no fertility treatment. After adjustment, children born after ART and OI/IUI had the same prevalence of being overweight (11%; POR 1.00, 95% confidence interval [CI] 0.91 to 1.11; p = 0.95) or obese (1.9%; POR 1.01, 95% CI 0.79 to 1.29; p = 0.94). Comparison of ICSI with conventional IVF yielded similar pattern (POR 0.95, 95% CI 0.83 to 1.07; p = 0.39 for overweight and POR 1.16, 95% CI 0.84 to 1.61; p = 0.36 for obesity). Obesity was more prevalent after frozen-thawed (2.7%) than fresh embryo transfer (1.8%) (POR 1.54, 95% CI 1.09 to 2.17; p = 0.01). The associations between fertility treatments and BMI were only modestly different in subgroups defined by the cause of infertility. Study limitations include potential residual confounding, restriction to live births, and lack of detailed technical information about the IVF procedures.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Laugesen, Kristina","Veres, Katalin","Hernandez-Diaz, Sonia","Chiu, Yu Han","Oberg, Anna Sara","Hsu, John","Rinaudo, Paolo","Spaan, Mandy","van Leeuwen, Flora","Sørensen, Henrik Toft"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-709653"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Laugesen, Kristina"],"country":["Netherlands","Sweden","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Harvard University","Karolinska Institutet","Netherlands Cancer Institute","Pennsylvania State University","University of California at San Francisco"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"overweight or obesity in children born after assisted reproductive technologies in denmark a population based cohort study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.1004324","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-709653","identifier":[{"id":"38113196","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10729995","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85180267193","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-ea1cff95-2529-4ae5-867d-390585d20540"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-827AEAE1D0DC5F1189262CDF9DA61A90"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.1004324","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Clinical Epidemiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"pennsylvania state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Pennsylvania State University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"se","key":"karolinska institutet","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska Institutet","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"university of california at san francisco","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California at San Francisco","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"nl","key":"netherlands cancer institute","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Netherlands Cancer Institute","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kristina","key":"laugesenpau","last":"Laugesen","locid":"ab62af11-21a5-4b44-a8ec-6c6d2afc742d","name":"Laugesen, Kristina","orcid":"0000-0001-7971-8223","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Katalin","key":"verespau","last":"Veres","locid":"6e08c83b-789f-4512-bb49-3e93a875f0d5","name":"Veres, Katalin","orcid":"0000-0001-8549-8781","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sonia","key":"hernandezdiazpau","last":"Hernandez-Diaz","name":"Hernandez-Diaz, Sonia","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Yu Han","key":"chiupau","last":"Chiu","name":"Chiu, Yu Han","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna Sara","key":"obergpau","last":"Oberg","name":"Oberg, Anna Sara","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"hsupau","last":"Hsu","name":"Hsu, John","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"rinaudopau","last":"Rinaudo","name":"Rinaudo, Paolo","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"Mandy","key":"spaanpau","last":"Spaan","name":"Spaan, Mandy","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"Flora","key":"vanleeuwenpau","last":"van Leeuwen","name":"van Leeuwen, Flora","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Toft","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"4d49d93a-4913-4223-ba59-272e717a845d","name":"Sørensen, Henrik Toft","orcid":"0000-0003-4299-7040","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38113196"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pmed.1004324","issn":["15491277","15491676"],"issn_org":["15491277"],"issue":"12","journal":"PLoS Medicine","paperid":"e1004324","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLoS Medicine","title_org":"PLOS Medicine","volume":"20"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85180267193"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-ea1cff95-2529-4ae5-867d-390585d20540"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Overweight or obesity in children born after assisted reproductive technologies in Denmark : A population-based cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-709653","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"The association between intrauterine growth restriction and cardiovascular disease (CVD) later in life might be confounded by familial factors. We conducted a binational register-based cohort study to assess associations of birth weight for gestational age (GA), a proxy for intrauterine growth restriction, and GA with CVD risk in early adulthood, before and after addressing familial factors via sibling comparison. We included 3,410,334 live nonmalformed singleton births from Sweden (1973-1996) and Denmark (1978-1998). During a median follow-up period of 10 years from age 18 years onwards, 29,742 individuals developed incident CVD (hypertension, ischemic heart disease, or cerebrovascular disease). Compared with individuals born with appropriate birth weight for GA (AGA; 10th-90th percentiles) or full term (39-40 gestational weeks), individuals born severely small for GA (SGA; ≤3rd percentile) or preterm (22-36 weeks) were at increased risk of CVD (hazard ratio (HR) = 1.38 (95% confidence interval (CI): 1.32, 1.45) and HR = 1.31 (95% CI: 1.25, 1.38), respectively). The association was attenuated when comparing individuals born SGA with their AGA siblings (HR = 1.11, 95% CI: 0.99, 1.25) but remained robust when comparing individuals born preterm with their term siblings (HR = 1.21, 95% CI: 1.07, 1.37). Our findings suggest that both SGA and preterm birth are associated with CVD risk in early adulthood, with greater familial confounding noted for SGA birth.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Lu, Donghao","Yu, Yongfu","Ludvigsson, Jonas F.","Oberg, Anna Sara","Sørensen, Henrik Toft","László, Krisztina D.","Li, Jiong","Cnattingius, Sven"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-688185"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Li, Jiong","Lu, Donghao"],"country":["China","Sweden","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Fudan University","Harvard University","Karolinska Institutet","Örebro University"],"keyword":["Cardiovascular disease","Cohort Studies","Fetal Growth Retardation","Preterm birth","Siblings"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"birth weight gestational age and risk of cardiovascular disease in early adulthood influence of familial factors","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/aje/kwac223","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-688185","identifier":[{"id":"36610737","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85160969717","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10236515","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-bbb3a00e-c210-4883-b5b0-e76c6e9275f5"],"keyword":["cardiovascular disease","cohort studies","fetal growth retardation","preterm birth","siblings"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-17B2B3D4B38205249899BDA4F4168481"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/aje/kwac223","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/401312412/kwac223.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"karolinska institutet","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska Institutet","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"örebro university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Örebro University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Clinical Epidemiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"cn","key":"fudan university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Fudan University","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Donghao","key":"lupau","last":"Lu","name":"Lu, Donghao","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Yongfu","key":"yupau","last":"Yu","locid":"06be533f-163e-454d-bd55-9219a4f5cafa","name":"Yu, Yongfu","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas F.","key":"ludvigssonpau","last":"Ludvigsson","name":"Ludvigsson, Jonas F.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna Sara","key":"obergpau","last":"Oberg","name":"Oberg, Anna Sara","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Toft","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"4d49d93a-4913-4223-ba59-272e717a845d","name":"Sørensen, Henrik Toft","orcid":"0000-0003-4299-7040","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Krisztina D.","key":"lászlópau","last":"László","name":"László, Krisztina D.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jiong","key":"lipau","last":"Li","locid":"304b9c99-9102-4963-8342-05aae67a5685","name":"Li, Jiong","orcid":"0000-0003-1125-026X","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sven","key":"cnattingiuspau","last":"Cnattingius","name":"Cnattingius, Sven","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36610737"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/aje/kwac223","issn":["00029262","14766256"],"issn_org":["00029262"],"issue":"6","journal":"American Journal of Epidemiology","pages":"866-877","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"American Journal of Epidemiology","volume":"192"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160969717"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-bbb3a00e-c210-4883-b5b0-e76c6e9275f5"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Birth Weight, Gestational Age, and Risk of Cardiovascular Disease in Early Adulthood : Influence of Familial Factors","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-688185","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Deadspace is the tissue and bony defect in a surgical wound after closure. This space is presumably poorly perfused favouring bacterial proliferation and biofilm formation. In arthroplasty surgery, an obligate deadspace surrounding the prosthesis is introduced and deadspace management, in combination with obtaining therapeutic prophylactic antibiotic concentrations, is important for limiting the risk of acquiring a periprosthetic joint infection (PJI). This study aimed to investigate cefuroxime distribution to an orthopaedic surgical deadspace in comparison with plasma and bone concentrations during two dosing intervals (8 h × 2). In a setup imitating shoulder arthroplasty surgery, but without insertion of a prosthesis, microdialysis catheters were placed for cefuroxime sampling in a deadspace in the glenohumeral joint and in cancellous bone of the scapular neck in eighteen pigs. Blood samples were collected as a reference. Cefuroxime was administered according to weight (20 mg/kg). The primary endpoint was time above the cefuroxime minimal inhibitory concentration of the free fraction of cefuroxime for Staphylococcus aureus (fT > MIC (4 μg/mL)). During the two dosing intervals, mean fT > MIC (4 μg/mL) was significantly longer in deadspace (605 min) compared with plasma (284 min) and bone (334 min). For deadspace, the mean time to reach 4 μg/mL was prolonged from the first dosing interval (8 min) to the second dosing interval (21 min), while the peak drug concentration was lower and half-life was longer in the second dosing interval. In conclusion, weight-adjusted cefuroxime fT > MIC (4 μg/mL) and elimination from the deadspace was longer in comparison to plasma and bone. Our results suggest a deadspace consolidation and a longer diffusions distance, resulting in a low cefuroxime turn-over. Based on theoretical targets, cefuroxime appears to be an appropriate prophylactic drug for the prevention of PJI.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Tøstesen, Sara Kousgaard","Stilling, Maiken","Hanberg, Pelle","Thillemann, Theis Muncholm","Falstie-Jensen, Thomas","Tøttrup, Mikkel","Knudsen, Martin","Petersen, Emil Toft","Bue, Mats"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-004915"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Tøstesen, Sara Kousgaard"],"keyword":["Antibiotic prophylaxis","Cefuroxime","Microdialysis","Orthopaedic surgical deadspace","Periprosthetic joint infection"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"high cefuroxime concentrations and long elimination in an orthopaedic surgical deadspace a microdialysis porcine study","src":["aau","au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/antibiotics11020208","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This research was funded by Novo Nordisk Foundation, grant number [NNF20OC0062032, 2020]."],"id":"S02-100.00-004915","identifier":[{"id":"35203810","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8868149","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85124619951","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-657530a5-1293-4a87-a4d7-ff50899d5751","au-a3a6e49b-5591-4559-9fea-8815186f8dd1"],"keyword":["Antibiotic prophylaxis","Cefuroxime","Microdialysis","Orthopaedic surgical deadspace","Periprosthetic joint infection"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-714FEF4DBB87CB03B3BFF19226AF9630"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/462276494/T_stesen_et_al._2022_._High_Cefuroxime_Concentrations_and_Long_Elimination_in_an_Orthopaedic_Surgical_Deadspace_A_Microdialysis_Porcine_Study.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/antibiotics11020208","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Orthopaedic surgery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Department of Orthopaedic Surgery","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Klinik Hoved-Orto","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sara Kousgaard","key":"tøstesenpau","last":"Tøstesen","locid":"fe641f6e-6c0d-4bcc-a6d7-862158d0831c","name":"Tøstesen, Sara Kousgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-2960-2342","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Maiken","key":"stillingpau","last":"Stilling","locid":"bb005d3f-27c9-4157-8101-db5059373d32","name":"Stilling, Maiken","orcid":"0000-0002-4530-2075","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Pelle","key":"hanbergpau","last":"Hanberg","locid":"242cb61c-bb82-4d48-9af7-8c84a17ab00e","name":"Hanberg, Pelle","orcid":"0000-0002-6845-1874","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Theis Muncholm","key":"thillemannpau","last":"Thillemann","locid":"1d931fc7-3227-4039-9753-f57097786b83","name":"Thillemann, Theis Muncholm","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Thomas","key":"falstiejensenpau","last":"Falstie-Jensen","locid":"d8cfd61b-fba5-45e5-bde3-dbf047fbe124","name":"Falstie-Jensen, Thomas","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Mikkel","key":"tøttruppau","last":"Tøttrup","locid":"fbe9b17b-1e56-4c25-9cc0-b59931569a32","name":"Tøttrup, Mikkel","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"knudsenpau","last":"Knudsen","locid":"2ea83f79-a875-4f1c-b1ff-12dc504ed698","name":"Knudsen, Martin Bruun","name_org":"Knudsen, Martin","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Emil Toft","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"9d0559d0-98b9-4c69-9aba-de57590aaf21","name":"Petersen, Emil Toft","orcid":"0000-0003-1697-8778","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mats","key":"buepau","last":"Bue","locid":"2a35a61c-3bf9-4bf7-9569-73178247b08c","name":"Bue, Mats","orcid":"0000-0001-7215-8323","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35203810"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/antibiotics11020208","issn":["20796382"],"issue":"2","journal":"Antibiotics","paperid":"208","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","status":"p","title":"Antibiotics","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85124619951"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-657530a5-1293-4a87-a4d7-ff50899d5751","au-a3a6e49b-5591-4559-9fea-8815186f8dd1"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University"],"title":"High Cefuroxime Concentrations and Long Elimination in an Orthopaedic Surgical Deadspace—A Microdialysis Porcine Study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-004915","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Disturbances in the brain fluid balance can lead to life-threatening elevation in the intracranial pressure (ICP), which represents a vast clinical challenge. Nevertheless, the details underlying the molecular mechanisms governing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) secretion are largely unresolved, thus preventing targeted and efficient pharmaceutical therapy of cerebral pathologies involving elevated ICP. METHODS: Experimental rats were employed for in vivo determinations of CSF secretion rates, ICP, blood pressure and ex vivo excised choroid plexus for morphological analysis and quantification of expression and activity of various transport proteins. CSF and blood extractions from rats, pigs, and humans were employed for osmolality determinations and a mathematical model employed to determine a contribution from potential local gradients at the surface of choroid plexus. RESULTS: We demonstrate that CSF secretion can occur independently of conventional osmosis and that local osmotic gradients do not suffice to support CSF secretion. Instead, the CSF secretion across the luminal membrane of choroid plexus relies approximately equally on the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter NKCC1, the Na+/HCO3- cotransporter NBCe2, and the Na+/K+-ATPase, but not on the Na+/H+ exchanger NHE1. We demonstrate that pharmacological modulation of CSF secretion directly affects the ICP. CONCLUSIONS: CSF secretion appears to not rely on conventional osmosis, but rather occur by a concerted effort of different choroidal transporters, possibly via a molecular mode of water transport inherent in the proteins themselves. Therapeutic modulation of the rate of CSF secretion may be employed as a strategy to modulate ICP. These insights identify new promising therapeutic targets against brain pathologies associated with elevated ICP.","author_count":16,"contributors":["Oernbo, Eva Kjer","Steffensen, Annette Buur","Khamesi, Pooya Razzaghi","Toft-Bertelsen, Trine L.","Barbuskaite, Dagne","Vilhardt, Frederik","Gerkau, Niklas J.","Tritsaris, Katerina","Simonsen, Anja H.","Lolansen, Sara Diana","Andreassen, Søren Norge","Hasselbalch, Steen Gregers","Zeuthen, Thomas","Rose, Christine R.","Kurtcuoglu, Vartan","MacAulay, Nanna"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-424074"],"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf","University Hospital Zürich"],"keyword":["ACTIVATED ADENOSINE TRIPHOSPHATASE","ALZHEIMERS ASSOCIATION WORKGROUPS","Bulk flow","Cerebral Ventricles","Cerebrospinal fluid","Choroid plexus","Choroid plexus epithelium","Cotransport","Diagnostic guidelines","Intracranial pressure","LATERAL INTERCELLULAR SPACES","NATIONAL INSTITUTE","Na K 2Cl cotransporter","Osmosis"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"membrane transporters control cerebrospinal fluid formation independently of conventional osmosis to modulate intracranial pressure","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12987-022-00358-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-424074","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9422132","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"36038945","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85137061356","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000847343000001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["ku-337fe2ee-1435-4265-b914-157c6f2f9858"],"keyword":["ACTIVATED ADENOSINE-TRIPHOSPHATASE","ALZHEIMERS ASSOCIATION WORKGROUPS","BULK FLOW","CEREBRAL-VENTRICLES","CHOROID-PLEXUS EPITHELIUM","Cerebrospinal fluid","Choroid plexus","Cotransport","DIAGNOSTIC GUIDELINES","Intracranial pressure","LATERAL INTERCELLULAR SPACES","NA+-K+-2CL(-) COTRANSPORTER","NATIONAL INSTITUTE","Osmosis","VENTRICULAR EPENDYMA"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-69F70FF4524D332FEAD160DF9216360F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/318707518/Fulltext.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12987-022-00358-4","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Neuroscience, Molecular Neuroscience"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ch","key":"university hospital zürich","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University Hospital Zürich","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"heinrich heine university düsseldorf","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Eva K.","key":"oernbopau","last":"Oernbo","locid":"56cbea28-f41a-421b-883d-29d830207e28","name":"Oernbo, Eva Kjer","name_org":"Oernbo, Eva K.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Annette B.","key":"steffensenpau","last":"Steffensen","locid":"a844e039-1c02-49c5-96af-10090530fff7","name":"Steffensen, Annette Buur","name_org":"Steffensen, Annette B.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Pooya Razzaghi","key":"khamesipau","last":"Khamesi","name":"Khamesi, Pooya Razzaghi","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Trine L.","key":"toftbertelsenpau","last":"Toft-Bertelsen","locid":"21214d2f-c04b-481e-81d6-dcc51234c0db","name":"Toft-Bertelsen, Trine L.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Dagne","key":"barbuskaitepau","last":"Barbuskaite","locid":"5d7c9154-da5b-4c1d-b7bc-3875a717c1db","name":"Barbuskaite, Dagne","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Frederik","key":"vilhardtpau","last":"Vilhardt","locid":"e4d61a4c-867d-4828-aa75-9f9c6e69a5fd","name":"Vilhardt, Frederik","orcid":"0000-0001-8604-7165","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Niklas J.","key":"gerkaupau","last":"Gerkau","name":"Gerkau, Niklas J.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Katerina","key":"tritsarispau","last":"Tritsaris","locid":"7dd8ec98-3340-46cc-bb11-dde6de86be8f","name":"Tritsaris, Katerina","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Anja H.","key":"simonsenpau","last":"Simonsen","name":"Simonsen, Anja H.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Sara D.","key":"lolansenpau","last":"Lolansen","locid":"f255e6e4-bc78-441b-acb8-eddb24c1732d","name":"Lolansen, Sara Diana","name_org":"Lolansen, Sara D.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Soren N.","key":"andreassenpau","last":"Andreassen","locid":"4dc31e1f-cb7c-44bd-a979-a4118879b801","name":"Andreassen, Søren Norge","name_org":"Andreassen, Soren N.","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Steen G.","key":"hasselbalchpau","last":"Hasselbalch","locid":"940c86d1-bb9d-49b6-b63a-bbfa2ed2d385","name":"Hasselbalch, Steen Gregers","name_org":"Hasselbalch, Steen G.","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas","key":"zeuthenpau","last":"Zeuthen","locid":"9a0f189b-e792-47f4-b344-466431f63e4b","name":"Zeuthen, Thomas","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Christine R.","key":"rosepau","last":"Rose","name":"Rose, Christine R.","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Vartan","key":"kurtcuoglupau","last":"Kurtcuoglu","name":"Kurtcuoglu, Vartan","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Nanna","key":"macaulaypau","last":"MacAulay","locid":"e172c907-dc29-4772-989d-c9cfae58765a","name":"MacAulay, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-7800-6600","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36038945"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12987-022-00358-4","issn":["20458118"],"issue":"1","journal":"Fluids and Barriers of the CNS","paperid":"65","status":"p","title":"Fluids and Barriers of the CNS","volume":"19"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85137061356"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-337fe2ee-1435-4265-b914-157c6f2f9858"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Membrane transporters control cerebrospinal fluid formation independently of conventional osmosis to modulate intracranial pressure","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-424074","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: A range of neurological pathologies may lead to secondary hydrocephalus. Treatment has largely been limited to surgical cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) diversion, as specific and efficient pharmacological options are lacking, partly due to the elusive molecular nature of the CSF secretion apparatus and its regulatory properties in physiology and pathophysiology. METHODS: CSF obtained from patients with subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) and rats with experimentally inflicted intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH) was analyzed for lysophosphatidic acid (LPA) by alpha-LISA. We employed the in vivo rat model to determine the effect of LPA on ventricular size and brain water content, and to reveal the effect of activation and inhibition of the transient receptor potential vanilloid 4 (TRPV4) ion channel on intracranial pressure and CSF secretion rate. LPA-mediated modulation of TRPV4 was determined with electrophysiology and an ex vivo radio-isotope assay was employed to determine the effect of these modulators on choroid plexus transport. RESULTS: Elevated levels of LPA were observed in CSF obtained from patients with subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) and from rats with experimentally-inflicted intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH). Intraventricular administration of LPA caused elevated brain water content and ventriculomegaly in experimental rats, via its action as an agonist of the choroidal transient receptor potential vanilloid 4 (TRPV4) channel. TRPV4 was revealed as a novel regulator of ICP in experimental rats via its ability to modulate the CSF secretion rate through its direct activation of the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC1) implicated in CSF secretion. CONCLUSIONS: Together, our data reveal that a serum lipid present in brain pathologies with hemorrhagic events promotes CSF hypersecretion and ensuing brain water accumulation via its direct action on TRPV4 and its downstream regulation of NKCC1. TRPV4 may therefore be a promising future pharmacological target for pathologies involving brain water accumulation.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Toft-Bertelsen, Trine L.","Barbuskaite, Dagne","Heerfordt, Eva Kjær","Lolansen, Sara Diana","Andreassen, Søren Norge","Rostgaard, Nina","Olsen, Markus Harboe","Norager, Nicolas H.","Capion, Tenna","Rath, Martin Fredensborg","Juhler, Marianne","MacAulay, Nanna"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-467540"],"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Bumetanide","Cerebrospinal fluid","Channel","Choroid plexus","GENES","Intraventricular hemorrhage","LPA","Membrane transport","Online","Ontology","Pathogenesis","Pathway","Pharmacology","SAH","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Transient receptor potential vanilloid 4","cotransporters"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"lysophosphatidic acid as a csf lipid in posthemorrhagic hydrocephalus that drives csf accumulation via trpv000000000004 induced hyperactivation of nkcc000000000001","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12987-022-00361-9","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-467540","identifier":[{"id":"000850576600002","type":"wos"},{"id":"85137309555","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36068581","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC9450297","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-7bb9dca3-f0c6-462d-90ac-7be543a9946b"],"keyword":["BUMETANIDE","Brain water","CHANNEL","COTRANSPORTERS","Cerebrospinal fluid","Choroid plexus","GENES","IVH","Intraventricular hemorrhage","LPA","Membrane transport","ONLINE","ONTOLOGY","PATHOGENESIS","PATHWAY","PHARMACOLOGY","SAH","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Transient receptor potential vanilloid 4"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-360D4A9C0C482FFBA0EFDFF116BB1037"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12987-022-00361-9","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/319359534/Fulltext.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Neuroscience, Molecular Neuroscience"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Trine Lisberg","key":"toftbertelsenpau","last":"Toft-Bertelsen","locid":"21214d2f-c04b-481e-81d6-dcc51234c0db","name":"Toft-Bertelsen, Trine L.","name_org":"Toft-Bertelsen, Trine Lisberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Dagne","key":"barbuskaitepau","last":"Barbuskaite","locid":"5d7c9154-da5b-4c1d-b7bc-3875a717c1db","name":"Barbuskaite, Dagne","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Eva Kjær","key":"heerfordtpau","last":"Heerfordt","name":"Heerfordt, Eva Kjær","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Sara Diana","key":"lolansenpau","last":"Lolansen","locid":"f255e6e4-bc78-441b-acb8-eddb24c1732d","name":"Lolansen, Sara Diana","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Søren Norge","key":"andreassenpau","last":"Andreassen","locid":"4dc31e1f-cb7c-44bd-a979-a4118879b801","name":"Andreassen, Søren Norge","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Nina","key":"rostgaardpau","last":"Rostgaard","locid":"bf128e4f-04a6-4a66-b948-240ff4d5b020","name":"Rostgaard, Nina","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Markus Harboe","key":"olsenpau","last":"Olsen","locid":"3ce2cba2-2ebf-4e0b-ba96-a8079ac43f1e","name":"Olsen, Markus Harboe","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Nicolas H.","key":"noragerpau","last":"Norager","name":"Norager, Nicolas H.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Tenna","key":"capionpau","last":"Capion","locid":"e44ef855-ec2d-43c1-805b-51f488badf9d","name":"Capion, Tenna","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Martin Fredensborg","key":"rathpau","last":"Rath","locid":"17e1980e-e336-4c55-ad3e-9e680dcd8364","name":"Rath, Martin Fredensborg","orcid":"0000-0002-4047-6324","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Marianne","key":"juhlerpau","last":"Juhler","locid":"84590289-5d53-45bc-a636-a35afb928caa","name":"Juhler, Marianne","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Nanna","key":"macaulaypau","last":"MacAulay","locid":"e172c907-dc29-4772-989d-c9cfae58765a","name":"MacAulay, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-7800-6600","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36068581"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12987-022-00361-9","issn":["20458118"],"issue":"1","journal":"Fluids and Barriers of the CNS","paperid":"69","status":"p","title":"Fluids and Barriers of the CNS","volume":"19"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85137309555"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-7bb9dca3-f0c6-462d-90ac-7be543a9946b"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Lysophosphatidic acid as a CSF lipid in posthemorrhagic hydrocephalus that drives CSF accumulation via TRPV4-induced hyperactivation of NKCC1","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-467540","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Importance: The diagnostic value is unclear of a 0 coronary artery calcium (CAC) score to rule out obstructive coronary artery disease (CAD) and near-term clinical events across different age groups. Objective: To assess the diagnostic value of a CAC score of 0 for reducing the likelihood of obstructive CAD and to assess the implications of such a CAC score and obstructive CAD across different age groups. Design, Setting, and Participants: This cohort study obtained data from the Western Denmark Heart Registry and had a median follow-up time of 4.3 years. Included patients were aged 18 years or older who underwent computed tomography angiography (CTA) between January 1, 2008, and December 31, 2017, because of symptoms that were suggestive of CAD. Data analysis was performed from April 5 to July 7, 2021. Exposures: Obstructive CAD, which was defined as 50% or more luminal stenosis. Main Outcomes and Measures: Proportion of individuals with obstructive CAD who had a CAC score of 0. Risk-adjusted diagnostic likelihood ratios were used to assess the diagnostic value of a CAC score of 0 for reducing the likelihood of obstructive CAD beyond clinical variables. Risk factors associated with myocardial infarction and death were estimated. Results: A total of 23 759 symptomatic patients, of whom 12 771 (54%) had a CAC score of 0, were included. This cohort had a median (IQR) age of 58 (49-65) years and was primarily composed of women (13 160 [55%]). Overall, the prevalence of obstructive CAD was relatively low across all age groups, ranging from 3% (39 of 1278 patients) in those who were younger than 40 years to 8% (52 of 619) among those who were 70 years or older. In patients with obstructive CAD, 14% (725 of 5043) had a CAC score of 0, and the prevalence varied across age groups from 58% (39 of 68) among those who were younger than 40 years, 34% (192 of 562) among those aged 40 to 49 years, 18% (268 of 1486) among those aged 50 to 59 years, 9% (174 of 1963) among those aged 60 to 69 years, to 5% (52 of 964) among those who were 70 years or older. The added diagnostic value of a CAC score of 0 decreased at a younger age, with a risk factor-adjusted diagnostic likelihood ratio of a CAC score of 0 ranging from 0.68 (approximately 32% lower likelihood of obstructive CAD than expected) in those who were younger than 40 years to 0.18 (approximately 82% lower likelihood than expected) in those who were 70 years or older. The presence of obstructive vs nonobstructive CAD among those with a CAC score of 0 was associated with a multivariable adjusted hazard ratio of 1.51 (95% CI, 0.98-2.33) for myocardial infarction and all-cause death; however, this hazard ratio varied from 1.80 (95% CI, 1.02-3.19) in those who were younger than 60 years to 1.24 (95% CI, 0.64-2.39) in those who were 60 years or older. Conclusions and Relevance: This cohort study found that the diagnostic value of a CAC score of 0 to rule out obstructive CAD beyond clinical variables was dependent on age, with the added diagnostic value being smaller for younger patients. In symptomatic patients who were younger than 60 years, a sizable proportion of obstructive CAD occurred among those without CAC and was associated with an increased risk of myocardial infarction and all-cause death.","author_count":20,"contributors":["Mortensen, Martin Bødtker","Gaur, Sara","Frimmer, Attila","Bøtker, Hans Erik","Sørensen, Henrik Toft","Kragholm, Kristian","Sand Rønnow, Niels Peter","Steffensen, Flemming Hald","Jensen, Rebekka Vibjerg","Mæng, Michael","Kanstrup, Helle","Blaha, Michael J.","Shaw, Leslee J.","Dzaye, Omar","Leipsic, Jonathon","Nørgaard, Bjarne Linde","Jensen, Jesper Møller"],"cuid":["S03-098.60-010957"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Mortensen, Martin Bødtker"],"country":["Canada","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Cornell University","Johns Hopkins University","St. Paul's Hospital","University of British Columbia","University of British Columbia (UBC)","University of California at Berkeley","Weill Cornell Medical College","Weill Cornell Medicine"],"keyword":["Absence","Aged","CT angiography","Calcification","Calcium metabolism","Cardiovascular disease","Computed Tomography Angiography","Computed Tomography Angiography methods","Coronary Stenosis diagnosis","Coronary Vessels diagnostic imaging","Denmark epidemiology","EVENTS","Female","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Morbidity trends","Predictive Value of Tests","Prevalence","Prognostic value","Registries","Risk Assessment methods","Risk Factors","Statin therapy","Survival Rate trends","Zero"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"association of age with the diagnostic value of coronary artery calcium score for ruling out coronary stenosis in symptomatic patients","src":["aau","au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8552116/?report=printable","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-098.60-010957","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8552116","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34705022","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85118561221","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000712536800004","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-e50eec36-6862-47fb-8a0b-4821500eb0c1","au-4b373beb-26d8-4766-ac6a-6347a42ae43e","sdu-80b3d097-d520-4102-8350-3902a12b116b"],"keyword":["ABSENCE","Aged","CALCIFICATION","CARDIOVASCULAR-DISEASE","COMPUTED-TOMOGRAPHY ANGIOGRAPHY","CT ANGIOGRAPHY","Calcium/metabolism","Computed Tomography Angiography